  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "Two resistances at $0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ with temperature coefficient of resistance $\\alpha_{1}$ and $\\alpha_{2}$ joined in series act as a single resistance in a circuit. The temperature coefficient of their single resistance will be",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "2Vku3",
      "text": "$\\alpha_{1}+\\alpha_{2}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "jCUnI",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha_{1} \\alpha_{2}}{\\alpha_{1}+\\alpha_{2}}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "soup6",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha_{1}-\\alpha_{2}}{2}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "MBbEX",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha_{1}+\\alpha_{2}}{2}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} R_{1} &=R_{0}\\left(1+\\alpha_{1} t\\right)+R_{0}\\left(1+\\alpha_{2} t\\right) \\\\ &=2 R_{0}\\left(1+\\frac{\\alpha_{1}+\\alpha_{2}}{2} t\\right) \\end{aligned}$\r\n\r\nComparing with $R=R_{0}(1+\\alpha t)$\r\n\r\n$\\alpha=\\frac{\\alpha_{1}+\\alpha_{2}}{2}$",
    "image": null
  },
